WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.410
Instructor: All right, so the next group

00:01.410 --> 00:04.140
of commands I'm gonna show you have to do with file

00:04.140 --> 00:08.700
and folder creation as well as deletion or destruction.

00:08.700 --> 00:10.963
So we've seen how to navigate around.

00:10.963 --> 00:12.750
But another really important piece

00:12.750 --> 00:14.615
is being able to make new files.

00:14.615 --> 00:16.860
So normally the way we would make a new file

00:16.860 --> 00:19.650
without the terminal would probably involve opening

00:19.650 --> 00:23.400
up some application like I use VS Code.

00:23.400 --> 00:25.440
I have it open, Visual Studio Code,

00:25.440 --> 00:28.982
and then I could go file, new or just command N

00:28.982 --> 00:31.080
and then start making a file.

00:31.080 --> 00:32.490
Whatever type of file this is,

00:32.490 --> 00:34.770
let's say it's a JavaScript file,

00:34.770 --> 00:36.840
I can put some JavaScript in it.

00:36.840 --> 00:40.470
Hello. It doesn't matter if you don't know JavaScript.

00:40.470 --> 00:42.160
And then I can save this file

00:42.160 --> 00:44.760
and then I pick a destination, right?

00:44.760 --> 00:49.663
And I give it a name like app.js

00:49.663 --> 00:50.880
with a .js extension

00:50.880 --> 00:53.066
and then I select somewhere for that to go.

00:53.066 --> 00:56.340
That's the typical workflow for creating a new file.

00:56.340 --> 00:58.064
If you're not doing it from a text editor

00:58.064 --> 01:00.990
or from some, you know, whatever Excel

01:00.990 --> 01:03.930
or Photoshop or something, you open an application

01:03.930 --> 01:06.083
and you create a file in the application.

01:06.083 --> 01:10.162
But instead we can use a command called touch.

01:10.162 --> 01:12.360
Touch, T-O-U-C-H.

01:12.360 --> 01:13.680
It doesn't actually stand for anything

01:13.680 --> 01:15.480
like the other commands where we have, you know,

01:15.480 --> 01:17.280
CD is change directory.

01:17.280 --> 01:18.791
Touch is just touch.

01:18.791 --> 01:21.600
It has a secondary use we're not really gonna go

01:21.600 --> 01:24.870
into where you can touch a file that already exists

01:24.870 --> 01:27.150
and you're simply updating the modified time.

01:27.150 --> 01:29.162
It really doesn't matter for now.

01:29.162 --> 01:31.890
If it helps, just think of it as a very weird name

01:31.890 --> 01:33.270
'cause it is very odd.

01:33.270 --> 01:34.140
If I were designing it,

01:34.140 --> 01:36.030
there'd be a command called I don't know,

01:36.030 --> 01:38.410
create or make or something.

01:38.410 --> 01:42.443
Anyway, so all that we need to do is run touch

01:42.443 --> 01:45.207
and then a space and then a file name

01:45.207 --> 01:47.730
or multiple that we want to create.

01:47.730 --> 01:50.880
And when we hit enter, wherever we currently are,

01:50.880 --> 01:54.020
whatever directory we're in, there will now be a file

01:54.020 --> 01:56.611
in this case called purple.txt.

01:56.611 --> 01:59.752
So I'll demonstrate this, I'll clear.

01:59.752 --> 02:02.761
And I'm currently in my home directory,

02:02.761 --> 02:05.959
so I can just make a file right here if I want to.

02:05.959 --> 02:09.423
Why don't I open finder just so you have it,

02:09.423 --> 02:10.800
so you can see it over there.

02:10.800 --> 02:13.230
I know it's not that easy to read.

02:13.230 --> 02:15.522
Unfortunately this is the largest I can make these icons

02:15.522 --> 02:17.122
but you're not missing much.

02:17.122 --> 02:21.213
Anyway, over here I'm going to create a file.

02:21.213 --> 02:23.250
What should we call this one?

02:23.250 --> 02:27.180
How about purple.js?

02:27.180 --> 02:30.419
So it's a JavaScript file called purple.

02:30.419 --> 02:32.730
So we don't really see anything change,

02:32.730 --> 02:37.110
but if I type LS now, we see a file that's been created

02:37.110 --> 02:41.962
and over here in my finder window I can see purple.js.

02:41.962 --> 02:45.240
So I can also make multiple at once.

02:45.240 --> 02:48.986
I could do red.txt for a text file

02:48.986 --> 02:53.986
and orange, what's another, .py for a Python file?

02:54.300 --> 02:56.430
I could even make a PDF file.

02:56.430 --> 02:58.680
These are all going to be completely empty,

02:58.680 --> 03:02.564
just an empty shell of a file with a name and an extension.

03:02.564 --> 03:06.413
So why don't I do yellow.pdf?

03:06.413 --> 03:09.588
That just made me three files in one go,

03:09.588 --> 03:11.340
red, orange, and yellow.

03:11.340 --> 03:13.139
I can see them here if I run LS

03:13.139 --> 03:15.544
or I could see them over here

03:15.544 --> 03:19.309
by inspecting them with my eyes, I guess.

03:19.309 --> 03:22.890
Navigating, you know, I can see them purple, orange,

03:22.890 --> 03:25.860
red and yellow.pdf.

03:25.860 --> 03:27.870
It is a true PDF file.

03:27.870 --> 03:30.201
My Mac is displaying the PDF icon.

03:30.201 --> 03:31.470
I can do the same thing

03:31.470 --> 03:34.602
of course over here on my Windows side.

03:34.602 --> 03:37.403
Let's see, we're just, whoops, LS.

03:37.403 --> 03:41.083
We're in the home directory. So I could make a file here.

03:41.083 --> 03:43.086
Let me clear this.

03:43.086 --> 03:46.833
Touch. How about magenta.txt?

03:47.924 --> 03:52.800
If I type LS, that will show up right there.

03:52.800 --> 03:56.163
And if I go to the file explorer, start.

03:57.630 --> 04:00.870
Here it is, magenta, and it's a totally empty file

04:00.870 --> 04:03.720
and I could open it from here, select what to open it with

04:03.720 --> 04:07.083
sure notepad, and then I can start working on it.

04:07.083 --> 04:09.090
But I don't have anything to work on,

04:09.090 --> 04:11.400
so I'm just gonna ignore that.

04:11.400 --> 04:14.023
Okay, so that's the basic concept of touch.

04:14.023 --> 04:16.165
The next thing I wanna show you is how

04:16.165 --> 04:18.930
we can make new folders.

04:18.930 --> 04:20.646
So touch is how we make new files.

04:20.646 --> 04:23.369
We can make as many as we want in a single go.

04:23.369 --> 04:27.186
Just separate the names by spaces, specify an extension,

04:27.186 --> 04:31.740
and those files will be created in your current directory.

04:31.740 --> 04:33.270
So I guess I should illustrate, you know,

04:33.270 --> 04:35.580
if I change directories into something else,

04:35.580 --> 04:38.790
for example, what should I do?

04:38.790 --> 04:40.410
Let's go on the Windows side.

04:40.410 --> 04:42.009
I don't have anything on my desktop.

04:42.009 --> 04:46.710
If I CD into desktop, I type LS, there's nothing there.

04:46.710 --> 04:51.433
But if I touch a file, how about touch cyan.js?

04:53.061 --> 04:55.610
Now I have a file on my desktop

04:55.610 --> 04:58.470
and I'm sure I could show that to you.

04:58.470 --> 05:01.528
Here it is, the only file on my desktop.

05:01.528 --> 05:05.287
So where I am when I run this command touch,

05:05.287 --> 05:08.160
that is where the file will be created.

05:08.160 --> 05:11.580
Although you can actually create a file somewhere else

05:11.580 --> 05:13.663
that you're currently not inside.

05:13.663 --> 05:16.770
For example, I could create a file inside of pets.

05:16.770 --> 05:18.342
I'm not in the pets directory.

05:18.342 --> 05:20.340
All I need to do is touch.

05:20.340 --> 05:23.381
And then the path to get to pets from where I am.

05:23.381 --> 05:24.990
I am in this folder.

05:24.990 --> 05:29.130
So I would reference pets, which is somewhere here.

05:29.130 --> 05:31.107
Where are you? There it is.

05:31.107 --> 05:34.523
And then maybe inside of horses,

05:34.523 --> 05:36.150
I could create a file.

05:36.150 --> 05:39.627
So I could do touch pets slash horses slash

05:39.627 --> 05:44.190
how about rocky dot, I don't know a PNG file?

05:44.190 --> 05:46.710
Which you normally wouldn't create an empty PNG,

05:46.710 --> 05:47.658
but there we go.

05:47.658 --> 05:50.340
And we see it created over here.

05:50.340 --> 05:53.520
I'm not gonna see it in my home directory.

05:53.520 --> 05:54.480
It's not here.

05:54.480 --> 05:57.840
I could CD into pets. I still won't see it.

05:57.840 --> 06:00.661
I could CD into horses and now I see it.

06:00.661 --> 06:04.290
All right. So that's the basics of making files.

06:04.290 --> 06:05.880
You can make them in a different location

06:05.880 --> 06:07.650
as long as you reference the correct path

06:07.650 --> 06:08.741
from where you are.

06:08.741 --> 06:11.850
Or you just make them in the current location where you are.

06:11.850 --> 06:13.586
Whatever you see when you type PWD,

06:13.586 --> 06:16.305
if you try and create a new file with touch,

06:16.305 --> 06:19.266
dot txt or whatever the file is,

06:19.266 --> 06:21.780
you're creating it in this location.

06:21.780 --> 06:22.830
And there we are.

06:22.830 --> 06:24.660
There's our second horse, good old, Rocky,

06:24.660 --> 06:27.207
and askldjalskdt.txt.

06:27.207 --> 06:28.860
Okay?

06:28.860 --> 06:31.770
The next thing we need to know how to do is make folders.

06:31.770 --> 06:32.826
So to make a directory,

06:32.826 --> 06:36.965
the command is make dear or make dir, I guess.

06:36.965 --> 06:40.290
I guess you pronounce it directory.

06:40.290 --> 06:43.140
So make dir. I always say make dear in my head.

06:43.140 --> 06:45.630
it doesn't matter. M-K-D-I-R.

06:45.630 --> 06:48.240
So this command, you follow it up with the name

06:48.240 --> 06:49.860
for directory you want to create.

06:49.860 --> 06:51.270
It works in the same way as touch.

06:51.270 --> 06:53.666
You can pass in multiple, separate them by spaces

06:53.666 --> 06:57.510
and by default they'll be created in your current location,

06:57.510 --> 06:58.934
in your working directory.

06:58.934 --> 07:01.770
So let's go to the pet's directory.

07:01.770 --> 07:04.440
Right now I'm in this horse's directory,

07:04.440 --> 07:07.804
so I'm gonna back out, okay?

07:07.804 --> 07:10.991
And I wanna make a new type of pet in this directory.

07:10.991 --> 07:13.650
So what should I do?

07:13.650 --> 07:16.170
Frogs? How about turtles?

07:16.170 --> 07:19.140
So we'll make a new directory, so not touch,

07:19.140 --> 07:21.510
but M-K-D-I-R turtles.

07:21.510 --> 07:22.950
And it doesn't have to be capitalized.

07:22.950 --> 07:25.686
I'm just doing that because all these other directories are

07:25.686 --> 07:29.813
and if I show you over here, there's no turtles folder

07:29.813 --> 07:33.146
but now there is and it's empty.

07:33.146 --> 07:36.030
So if I type LS, I see turtles.

07:36.030 --> 07:39.840
I can CD into turtles. I can create a file in here.

07:39.840 --> 07:41.972
What's a famous turtle?

07:41.972 --> 07:44.343
I guess there's Lonesome George,

07:44.343 --> 07:45.990
who technically is a tortoise,

07:45.990 --> 07:47.580
but we'll make a file for him.

07:47.580 --> 07:50.605
How about a Python file? Great.

07:50.605 --> 07:53.280
And then I could even make another folder in here.

07:53.280 --> 07:54.729
Which should I call this folder?

07:54.729 --> 07:57.823
Inside of turtles, let's make two directories.

07:57.823 --> 08:00.585
How about sea turtles?

08:00.585 --> 08:03.090
And this is actually an important note.

08:03.090 --> 08:06.388
You don't wanna put spaces in your names of files.

08:06.388 --> 08:09.444
We want to, especially if we're using make directory,

08:09.444 --> 08:12.930
it's gonna think this is two directories we want to make.

08:12.930 --> 08:14.303
Sea space turtles.

08:14.303 --> 08:17.827
If you really wanted a space, you could do this with quotes

08:17.827 --> 08:20.130
but I don't recommend having spaces in there,

08:20.130 --> 08:21.600
it just causes problems.

08:21.600 --> 08:23.730
So do sea dash turtles?

08:23.730 --> 08:24.960
Sea turtles, like this?

08:24.960 --> 08:27.390
See underscore turtles, it doesn't matter.

08:27.390 --> 08:29.040
So I'll make a directory for sea turtles

08:29.040 --> 08:32.103
and then we'll just call them land turtles.

08:33.630 --> 08:36.030
Sure. I don't think that's the right term actually.

08:36.030 --> 08:37.650
But here we are.

08:37.650 --> 08:41.043
I now have two directories and a file inside of turtles.

08:42.150 --> 08:44.160
So two empty folders I just made,

08:44.160 --> 08:45.818
and then I can fill out their contents.

08:45.818 --> 08:49.170
So that's make directory, you just pass in a name.

08:49.170 --> 08:51.356
I'll quickly demonstrate this on a Windows machine.

08:51.356 --> 08:54.330
I'm on the desktop. I'll make a folder here.

08:54.330 --> 08:55.710
M-K-D-I-R.

08:55.710 --> 08:58.299
How about, I don't know, cars?

08:58.299 --> 09:02.823
And I'll do two at a time. Cars and bikes.

09:03.876 --> 09:05.601
And then if I want to view them,

09:05.601 --> 09:08.100
I could do LS, I see them, right?

09:08.100 --> 09:10.800
And they're visually represented differently,

09:10.800 --> 09:11.940
a different color.

09:11.940 --> 09:14.378
And then also a slash after their name.

09:14.378 --> 09:16.703
But I could also view my desktop.

09:16.703 --> 09:20.578
And then I see the two empty folders plus my cyan file.

09:20.578 --> 09:24.960
Okay, so that is make directory as well as touch.

09:24.960 --> 09:27.390
Those are the two commands we saw in this video.

09:27.390 --> 09:30.374
Just remember that they run relative to where you are.

09:30.374 --> 09:33.630
So if you wanna make a file in some folder,

09:33.630 --> 09:36.300
it's easiest if you navigate to that folder.

09:36.300 --> 09:39.180
And you can make a hundred files at once using touch

09:39.180 --> 09:41.310
and then open up an editor or something

09:41.310 --> 09:43.350
to actually work on those files.

09:43.350 --> 09:45.210
Okay, so next up,

09:45.210 --> 09:47.943
the last piece we're gonna talk about deleting stuff.
